\begin{abstract}
This paper compares different machine learning approaches for image classification tasks. We evaluate several methods including KNN with HOG features, Random Forest, and hybrid approaches that combine traditional and deep learning techniques like CNN+KNN and XGBoost+CNN. Our results show that hybrid approaches perform better than traditional methods alone, with CNN+KNN and XGBoost+CNN achieving 44.7\% and 44.5\% accuracy respectively. These findings provide valuable insights for developing effective image classification systems, especially when dealing with limited training data or computational constraints.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Problem Statement}
This research investigates the effectiveness of various machine learning approaches for image classification tasks, with a focus on hybrid methods that combine traditional and deep learning techniques. Our main goal is to determine which approaches offer the best performance in terms of classification accuracy, computational efficiency, and model interpretability.

Key research questions include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item How do traditional feature extraction methods compare to representations learned from CNNs?
    \item Can hybrid models combine the strengths of both traditional machine learning and deep learning?
    \item How do different approaches perform with varying amounts of training data and computational resources?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Related Work}
The field of image classification has evolved significantly over time. Traditional approaches used handcrafted features like Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG) combined with classical algorithms such as Support Vector Machines (SVM), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), and Random Forests.

The rise of deep learning, especially Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), changed the field dramatically. Models like ResNet learn features directly from data, eliminating the need for manual feature engineering.

Recent research has explored hybrid approaches that combine deep learning with traditional machine learning. Wang et al. showed that CNN features combined with SVM classifiers can outperform end-to-end CNN models on certain datasets. These hybrid approaches are particularly valuable when training data or computational resources are limited.

\section{Approaches Tried}
\subsection{Traditional Machine Learning Approaches}
\subsubsection{KNN with HOG Features}
The k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) algorithm combined with Histogram of Oriented Gradients (HOG) features is a classical approach to image classification.

\textbf{HOG Feature Extraction:} We implemented HOG feature extraction following below approach:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Dividing images into $8 \times 8$ pixel cells
    \item Computing gradient magnitude and orientation for each pixel
    \item Creating histogram of gradient orientations for each cell
    \item Normalizing histograms within $16 \times 16$ pixel blocks
    \item Concatenating normalized histograms to form the final feature vector
\end{itemize}

\textbf{KNN Classification:} We implemented the k-Nearest Neighbors algorithm using Euclidean distance. After testing different parameter values, we selected k = 5 as the optimal number of neighbors. We applied distance weighting where each neighbor's vote is weighted by the inverse of its distance to the query image.

This approach achieved an accuracy of 26.5\% on our test set, providing a baseline for comparison with more advanced methods.

\subsubsection{Random Forest}
We implemented a Random Forest classifier using color and texture features:

\textbf{Feature Extraction:} For each image, we extracted:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Various color space histograms
    \item Local Binary Patterns
    \item Haralick texture features (energy, entropy, contrast, homogeneity)
    \item Hu moments for shape characterization
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Random Forest Configuration:}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Number of trees: 500
    \item Maximum depth: 30
    \item Minimum samples per leaf: 5
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Feature Importance Analysis:} Our analysis showed that texture features were the most useful for classification.

This approach achieved an accuracy of 39.17\% on our test set, significantly outperforming the KNN+HOG approach. The better performance can be attributed to the ensemble nature of Random Forests and their ability to handle high-dimensional feature spaces effectively.

\subsection{Hybrid Approaches}
\subsubsection{CNN with Naive Bayes}
We implemented a hybrid approach combining CNN feature extraction with a Naive Bayes classifier:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Feature Extraction:} We used ResNet50 to extract feature vectors from each image.
    \item \textbf{Feature Discretization:} Since Naive Bayes works best with discrete features, we divided the continuous CNN features into 10 equal-frequency bins.
    \item \textbf{Naive Bayes Classification:} We trained a Multinomial Naive Bayes classifier on the discretized features.
\end{itemize}

This approach achieved an accuracy of 37.0\% on our test set. While lower than the Random Forest baseline, it demonstrates the potential of combining deep learning feature extraction with probabilistic classifiers.

\subsubsection{XGBoost with CNN Features}
We implemented a hybrid approach combining CNN feature extraction with Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Feature Extraction:} We used ResNet50 to extract feature vectors from each image.
    \item \textbf{Feature Normalization:} We standardized the features to have zero mean and unit variance.
    \item \textbf{XGBoost Classification:} We trained an XGBoost classifier with the following parameters:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Number of estimators: 200
        \item Maximum depth: 6
        \item Learning rate: 0.1
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

This approach achieved an accuracy of 44.5\% on our test set, representing a significant improvement over previous methods. The superior performance can be attributed to XGBoost's ability to build complex decision boundaries in the feature space derived from CNN embeddings.

\subsubsection{CNN with KNN}
We implemented another hybrid approach combining CNN feature extraction with k-Nearest Neighbors classification:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Feature Extraction:} We used ResNet50 to extract feature vectors from each image.
    \item \textbf{Dimensionality Reduction:} We applied dimensionality reduction techniques to reduce the feature dimensionality to 50, preserving the local structure of the feature space.
    \item \textbf{KNN Classification:} We implemented k-Nearest Neighbors classification with:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Number of neighbors: 7
        \item Distance metric: Cosine similarity
        \item Weighting: Distance-weighted voting
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

This approach achieved the highest accuracy of 44.7\% on our test set, slightly outperforming the XGBoost+CNN approach. The effectiveness of this method comes from combining CNNs (for feature extraction) with KNN (for flexible decision boundaries based on similarity in the reduced feature space).

\section{Experimental Setup}
\subsection{Dataset}
We conducted our experiments on the Caltech-101 dataset, which contains 9,144 images across 101 object categories and a background class. The dataset presents significant challenges due to variations within classes, diverse backgrounds, and uneven class distributions.

We preprocessed the dataset by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Resizing all images to $128 \times 128$ pixels while preserving aspect ratio
    \item Normalizing pixel values to the range [0, 1]
    \item Splitting the dataset into training (70\%), validation (15\%), and test (15\%) sets
    \item Using stratified sampling to maintain class distributions across splits
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Details}
All experiments were implemented using Python 3.8 with libraries including TensorFlow, Scikit-learn, XGBoost, OpenCV, NumPy, and SciPy.

For CNN training, we used the following optimization parameters:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Optimizer: Adam with learning rate 0.001
    \item Batch size: 64
    \item Early stopping with patience of 10 epochs
    \item Maximum epochs: 100
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
We evaluated the performance of all approaches using:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Accuracy: The proportion of correctly classified images
    \item Precision, Recall, and F1-Score: Calculated for each class and averaged
    \item Confusion Matrix: To analyze per-class performance and common misclassifications
    \item Top-5 Accuracy: Whether the correct label is among the top 5 predicted labels
    \item Inference Time: The average time required for classifying a single image
    \item Model Size: The disk space required to store the trained model
\end{itemize}

