The results demonstrate several key insights:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Traditional machine learning approaches (KNN+HOG) significantly underperform compared to hybrid approaches, highlighting the limitations of handcrafted features for complex image classification.
    \item Random Forest demonstrates competitive performance despite using traditional features, showing the power of ensemble learning even with relatively simple features.
    \item Hybrid approaches consistently outperform traditional methods, with CNN+KNN and XGBoost+CNN achieving the highest accuracies.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}
For the XGBoost+CNN approach, we conducted feature importance analysis to identify the most discriminative CNN features. The analysis revealed that features from the middle convolutional layers contributed most significantly to classification performance. These features capture medium-level abstractions such as textures and part detectors.

For the Random Forest approach, we analyzed the importance of different feature types:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Texture features: 35\% contribution
    \item Haralick features: 18\% contribution
    \item Hu moments: 5\% contribution
\end{itemize}

This analysis highlights the importance of texture information for traditional machine learning approaches.

\subsection{Hyperparameter Sensitivity}
For CNN+KNN, we analyzed the effect of the number of neighbors (k) and dimensionality reduction techniques. The results showed that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Performance peaked at k = 7 and declined for larger values
    \item Dimensionality reduction to 50 dimensions provided the best balance between information preservation and noise reduction
    \item Cosine similarity performed better than Euclidean distance
\end{itemize}

For XGBoost+CNN, the most sensitive hyperparameters were:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Number of estimators (optimal: 200)
    \item Maximum depth (optimal: 6)
    \item Learning rate (optimal: 0.1)
\end{itemize}

