\subsection{Error Analysis}
Our detailed error analysis revealed several patterns in misclassifications across different methods:

\textbf{Visual Similarity Confusion:} A major source of errors occurred between visually similar categories. For example:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Different species of birds were frequently confused with each other
    \item Similar vehicle types (sedans vs. coupes, SUVs vs. minivans) showed high confusion rates
    \item Musical instruments with similar shapes (violin vs. cello, trumpet vs. French horn) were often misclassified
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Intra-class Variation:} Categories with high internal variation posed significant challenges:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The "background" class, which contained diverse images, had the highest error rates
    \item Classes with significant pose variations (e.g., animals photographed from different angles)
    \item Objects with variable appearances (e.g., buildings, furniture) showed inconsistent classification
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Training Data Imbalance:} Categories with limited training examples (fewer than 50 images) showed substantially higher error rates. The models struggled with underrepresented classes, often misclassifying them as more common categories.

\textbf{Background Interference:} Objects photographed against complex or unusual backgrounds were more frequently misclassified, as the models sometimes focused on background elements rather than the primary object.

\textbf{Scale and Occlusion Issues:} Partially occluded objects or objects photographed at unusual scales had higher misclassification rates across all methods.

Different approaches showed complementary error patterns. CNN+KNN performed better on texture-rich categories (like fabrics, plants, and food items), while XGBoost+CNN excelled at object categories with distinctive shapes (like vehicles, electronics, and furniture).

\section{Summary and Future Work}
This paper presented a comprehensive evaluation of machine learning approaches for image classification, focusing on hybrid methodologies that combine traditional and deep learning techniques. Our experiments demonstrated that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Hybrid approaches consistently outperform both traditional machine learning and standalone methods, with CNN+KNN and XGBoost+CNN achieving the highest accuracies.
    \item Traditional feature engineering approaches still provide value when combined with appropriate classifiers, as shown by the strong performance of Random Forest.
    \item Different approaches have varying computational requirements, presenting important trade-offs between accuracy, training time, inference speed, and model size.
\end{itemize}

These findings have important implications for practical image classification systems, particularly when training data or computational resources are limited. By combining the strengths of different machine learning approaches, hybrid methods can achieve robust performance across diverse image classification tasks.

Future research directions include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Investigating more sophisticated feature fusion techniques
    \item Exploring approaches for categories with limited training examples
    \item Extending the evaluation to more complex datasets and real-world applications
    \item Using self-supervised learning to improve feature representations with limited labeled data
\end{itemize}

